Subject: Re: possible pkgsrc emacs framework improvements?
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

(DRAFT -- not sent.)

Hi Thomas,

Thank you for reading it through, and for the three-way split -- it
is a better shape than the one I had, and it turned out to draw
itself once I asked of each change "is this wrong on trunk today,
with the modules.mk that is there now?".  So below are the three
parts, each with its diff, in that order: the framework, then the
fixes for what is already broken, then the adaptations that only
matter once the framework is in.  The fixes and the adaptations do
not depend on each other, and neither depends on the framework to
apply; the fixes are the part I would think of for the freeze, if any
of it is, since they are wrong now and stay wrong either way.

I understand about mk/ waiting.  I have kept going in the meantime,
and this is where it has got to.  I am not out of steam yet, so if
there is a part you would rather take yourself, say so and I will
stop touching it.

Everything here is on the branch as well, in the same three
directories:

  https://github.com/zakinko/pkgsrc-zakinko/tree/emacs-framework-3parts/emacs-framework

All the diffs apply to trunk as of 2026-09-18 (c8e5216), in the order
given, in the reverse order, part by part in either order, with no
offset and no fuzz.  I built with them on NetBSD 11.0/amd64; the CI
copy runs on i386 11.0 against the same trunk files.


1. The framework
================

modules.mk, mk/pbulk/pbulk-index.mk, and four small diffs to the
Emacs packages themselves (version.mk, buildlink3.mk, and a
site-start.el they now install).  What it does, in the order of your
three points, and then what building with it added.

Version selection is pyversion.mk's, including the part I had got
wrong the first time.  EMACS_VERSION_REQD is honoured or refused;
otherwise EMACS_TYPE is a default, and a package that does not accept
it falls back to the first version it does accept, walking the
package's EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED in the order the package wrote it.
(I had said that last part was done, and it was the intent, not the
code: the loop walked modules.mk's list, so mailcrypt writing emacs20
first still got emacs31.  It walks the package's now, and reordering
moves the answer only where nothing else decides it.)
EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE, which devel/apel sets and nothing read,
is subtracted the way pyversion.mk subtracts its equivalent.  And the
dependency pattern is read back through PKGNAME_REQD, which is what
lets a chain agree with itself: www/emacs-w3m accepts emacs20 and
devel/apel does not, so with EMACS_TYPE=emacs20 emacs-w3m used to ask
for emacs20-apel while apel built itself as emacs29-apel, and nothing
said why.  Now the build stops at apel with the version, the package
and the accepted list named.

The lisp goes to share/emacs/<version>/site-lisp.  Emacs already
searches there and its first-level subdirectories, so nothing has to
be told; I installed everything that builds, listed all 390 .elc
files they own, and each is on load-path.  share/emacs/site-lisp is
still beside them, so a package that has not moved is unaffected.

The name carries the version: emacs29-apel, emacs30-apel, and only
the version.  The nox build and the X build of one Emacs conflict
with each other, share the same site-lisp, and no package in the tree
installs a different file list for the two -- I went through the 87
conditions on it -- so one emacs30-foo serves either, and pbulk makes
one of it rather than two: devel/apel goes from six entries to three,
textproc/xslide from eleven to six.  The dependency is written so
either build satisfies it, and pkg_add enforces it: with only
emacs30-nox11 installed, the same binary package installs or refuses
according to that one line.

  @pkgdep emacs30>=30.1<31              pkg_add: no pkg found, sorry.
  @pkgdep {emacs30-nox11,emacs30}>=...  installs

A package that refuses the X build -- says so in
EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE -- is the exception, and is named
emacs30-nox11-foo with its dependency pinned to that build, so a
binary package cannot land on an Emacs that cannot run it.  With
EMACS_TYPE=emacs30nox and devel/apel:

  modules.mk as it is  apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2, and the same again
                       when told EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE=emacs30nox
  with the line read   emacs30-apel        by default
                       emacs29-apel        told emacs30nox is no good
                       emacs30-nox11-apel  told emacs30 is no good

XEmacs stays split by series, xemacs214- and xemacs215-, which today
are both xemacs-.  Packages stop having to spell both halves of every
pair in EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED, though the lists that do still work.

pbulk is four lines rather than the three I said: the class also has
to be named in _PBULK_MULTI, which is the list the loop walks, and it
wants the filtered list rather than the written one, for the reason
python takes _PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED.  With them devel/apel gives

  PKGNAME=emacs30-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs29-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs31-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2

with the default first; devel/pcl-cvs, which takes emacs20 and
nothing else, gives one line; devel/gmake, which never includes
modules.mk, is untouched.

Then three things that building found, all invisible while only one
Emacs can be installed.

EMACS_BIN was ${PREFIX}/bin/${_EMACS_FLAVOR}, so building for emacs29
byte-compiled with whichever Emacs owned bin/emacs, which was 30.2.
It is now the versioned binary for GNU Emacs.  XEmacs keeps the plain
name on purpose: 21.5 installs bin/xemacs-21.5-b36 but 21.4 installs
no versioned executable at all, only a bin/xemacs-21.4.25-<hash>.dmp.
I built 21.4 to find that out rather than guess a name.

Each nox version.mk was a copy of its X11 twin with one line changed,
and the copies had rotted: editors/emacs29-nox11/version.mk said 29.1
while the package builds 29.4, so the lisp went to
share/emacs/29.1/site-lisp and the build's load path pointed at a
directory that does not exist.  The nox file now includes its twin
and sets only what differs, and the ABI floor that each buildlink3.mk
wrote a third time has moved into version.mk, so the version is
written in one place per Emacs.

Info and etc move under the version with the lisp, since with the
version in the name two builds of one package are two packages that
write the same file, and pkg_add stops the second:

  pkg_add: emacs29-skk-17.1nb2: conflicting PLIST with
           emacs30-skk-17.1nb2: info/skk.info

That is inputmethod/skk built twice, once against each Emacs; 30 of
the 87 install info.  For etc it is share/emacs/<version>/etc, safe
because the two packages that use it bake the path in at build time.
For info I have gone through PKGINFODIR rather than a path of the
framework's own: modules.mk sets it to share/emacs/<version>/info
once the version is known, and everything pkgsrc already does for
info files -- the info/ canonicalisation in plist-info.awk, the
--infodir that gnu-configure.mk passes, the dir file -- follows
without a package being told.  A PLIST that spells info/foo.info is
still right.  I tried the other way first, a path of its own with
eleven PLISTs rewritten to ${EMACS_INFOPREFIX}/, and it was worse in
every direction: mail/mailutils passes --infodir=${PREFIX}/${PKGINFODIR}
and so wrote to the old place while its PLIST looked in the new, and
devel/cqual the opposite.  With PKGINFODIR neither needs a line.

Info does need a receiving half, unlike the lisp: Emacs finds it
through Info-default-directory-list, built from its own --infodir at
configure time, and the only runtime lever is INFOPATH.  So the Emacs
packages now install a two-line site-start.el beside their site-lisp:

  (add-to-list (quote Info-default-directory-list)
    (expand-file-name "../info" (file-name-directory load-file-name)) t)

and skk installs share/emacs/30.2/info/skk.info and C-h i finds it;
without the file it does not.  It takes over site-start.el, which is
a name an administrator may want -- a site-start.d read by a one-line
site-start.el would leave that free, and I did not want to invent a
convention for the tree without asking.  Which packages I built with
the info change, and which are still unmeasured, is under 4.

One more that was mine: EMACSLOADPATH for the build still named the
old shared site-lisp, so anything byte-compiling against another
elisp package stopped with "Cannot open load file".  Only building
found it; EMACS_LISPPREFIX was right all along.

What this part does not do is let two Emacsen coexist.  editors/emacs29
and editors/emacs30 declare no conflict with each other and share 93
files -- bin/emacs, bin/ctags, bin/etags, bin/emacsclient,
bin/ebrowse, include/emacs-module.h, 66 info files, 15 under share --
so pkg_add stops the second one over bin/ctags.  With those 93 files
removed by hand the two run side by side, emacs 29.4 and emacs 30.2,
and everything above was measured that way.  Versioning the binaries
the way python313 does is a bigger change than the three you asked
about and I have not made it; it does set the ceiling on what the
per-version name buys until it is made: a bulk build can make every
version and the name records which Emacs a binary package is for, but
two of them still cannot sit on one machine.

Two costs of the prefix that I would rather put in front of you than
decide.  It takes a package out of the vulnerability list's reach:
pkg_admin audit-pkg reports CAN-2004-0422 for flim-1.14.2 and nothing
for emacs30-flim-1.14.2 -- or for xemacs-flim-1.14.2, so this is
already true of the xemacs- packages today.  An advisory can cover it
by enumerating, {,emacs30-}flim<1.14.3, and 3778 of the 30234 entries
in the list already open with a brace; nothing currently in it is
affected, since the only advisory for an elisp package rather than
for Emacs itself is flim<1.14.3 and pkgsrc has 1.14.9.  And CONFLICTS
patterns that name a renamed package stop matching -- that one is in
the fixes, where it bit.

Diffs: modules.mk (402 lines against 1.42), pbulk-index.mk (20), and
the four to the Emacs packages.

[framework diffs]


2. Bug fixes -- wrong on trunk today
=====================================

Twenty packages.  I have grouped them by what is wrong, and each was
built with its diff under the framework; the ones marked (x) were
also built against your modules.mk with an xemacs-nox11 21.5.36, since
XEmacs already has a prefix and shows them today.

Cannot be built at all:

  cad/dinotrace-mode   EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED names emacs26, which is
                       gone.  Costs nothing while emacs29 is also in
                       the list, which is why it has sat there.  I
                       built it with emacs29 alone before narrowing
                       it, since the comment above worries about
                       athena widgets: it builds against
                       emacs29-nox11, which has no toolkit, and loads.
  devel/semi           the semi-bbdb option includes
                       misc/bbdb2/buildlink3.mk, and bbdb2 went with
                       emacs21, so the option fails to parse.
                       Pointing it at bbdb3 was my first try and is
                       wrong: mime-bbdb.el is BBDB 2 code and
                       byte-compiles with six functions undefined,
                       none of which bbdb-3.2.2a has under any name.
                       The diff takes the option out; what the
                       feature needs is a port of mime-bbdb.el.
  mail/mu              PLIST.emacs lists mu4e-dbus.elc unconditionally
                       while mu4e/meson.build only byte-compiles it
                       when the Emacs has dbusbind, so it cannot be
                       packaged against any nox Emacs.  The guard is
                       not needed -- both requires pass 'noerror, and
                       against emacs30-nox11 the .elc builds and
                       loads -- so the patch drops it.  Nobody builds
                       mu against a nox Emacs today, which is why
                       nobody has seen it.  Its -Dinfodir=info is in
                       the same diff, for the reason under 4.

Declarations that do not match what they name:

  editors/gnuserv (x)  CONFLICTS names xemacs-current-[0-9]*, which
                       matches nothing, and xemacs-[0-9]*, which
                       misses the nox packages.  I found it by having
                       it happen: pkg_add refused xemacs-nox11 over
                       bin/gnuattach instead of the line refusing it
                       first.  Also share/doc/${PKGBASE} in
                       INSTALLATION_DIRS while doc-install writes
                       share/doc/gnuserv, so under xemacs that name
                       becomes a 484-byte file holding whichever
                       README went last.
  textproc/dictem (x)  the same pair: installs share/doc/xemacs-dictem
                       while the PLIST asks for share/doc/dictem.
  inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi
                       CONFLICTS= tamago-[0-9]* does not match
                       emacs20-tamago-20020909nb5, so two packages
                       that select emacs20 land in the same egg
                       directory with nothing declared between them.
                       With the prefix in the pattern it matches
                       emacs20-tamago while tamago-tsunagi is built
                       for emacs20 and not under emacs30, which is
                       right: they only collide when they share a
                       lisp directory.

Wrong under XEmacs today:

  editors/matlab-mode (x)
  textproc/xslide (x)  both stop at the byte-compile, "Cannot open
                       load file: tempo" and "sendmail".  Neither
                       library is in XEmacs; both are in
                       editors/xemacs-packages and neither package
                       depends on it -- devel/doxymacs and
                       textproc/migemo-elisp carry that line in their
                       xemacs branch, so there was a precedent to
                       copy.  Past that both hit a ${PKGBASE} pair:
                       matlab-mode creates
                       ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE:C|^xemacs-||} and
                       installs into ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE},
                       xslide installs into ${PKGBASE} while its PLIST
                       says xslide.  With both fixes they build:
                       xemacs-matlab-mode-2.3.1nb4, xemacs-xslide-0.2.2nb4.

Wrong under every current GNU Emacs today, and the one I would put
first:

  lang/eieio           has no EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED, so it builds
                       for emacs30, and eieio has been part of GNU
                       Emacs since 24.1.  site-lisp comes before
                       lisp/ on load-path, so with emacs30-eieio-0.17
                       installed (require 'eieio) loads the 2004 copy
                       and eieio-make-class-predicate is void; mail/mu
                       then fails to build its man pages through
                       org-export, and earlier I had blamed
                       markdown-mode and rmail-mime for wanting that
                       function "which this Emacs does not have" --
                       it has it, eieio 0.17 was hiding it.  The same
                       is true of share/emacs/site-lisp today.  The
                       diff limits it to emacs20 and XEmacs, which do
                       not bundle it (xemacs-packages carries one);
                       devel/semantic is its only dependent and
                       accepts exactly those.

Not using the framework -- the path is right today only because it
happens to coincide with the variable, and the tree already has the
variable for exactly this:

  devel/cqual          bundled makefile decides lispdir; the PLIST
                       spells share/emacs/site-lisp/cqual.  Both
                       lines, because with only one the two halves
                       split.  This one and tamago-tsunagi build green
                       into the old directory under the framework
                       with no fix, which I think is the worst shape.
  editors/tamago       bundled configure decides lispdir.
  inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi
                       bundled makefile; the PLIST spells the path.
  devel/pcl-cvs        bundled makefile; there is no "lispdir" in the
                       package Makefile to grep for, only building
                       finds it.
  devel/rsltc          its own Makefile spells
                       ${PREFIX}/share/emacs/site-lisp/rsl three
                       times, and the PLIST too.
  net/twittering-mode
  textproc/emacs-dict-client
  mail/mailutils       PLIST spells the path; the install already
                       follows the variable.
  misc/emacs-wiki      passes no ELISPDIR to its install; and asks for
                       semi>=1.14.6 without the prefix, which can
                       never be satisfied once semi is renamed.
  devel/flim           adds an unprefixed apel>=10.8 to
                       BUILDLINK_API_DEPENDS.apel, beside apel's own
                       ${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}apel>=10.  Under xemacs
                       that already asks for two different packages.
  www/emacs-w3m        passes no --with-lispdir, so the install writes
                       the old shared site-lisp while the PLIST
                       follows the variable; and --infodir=${PREFIX}/info
                       where the tree writes ${PKGINFODIR}.  I had
                       earlier read its WRKSRC=${WRKDIR}/${PKGBASE}-...
                       as a ${PKGBASE} problem; it is not, because
                       emacs-w3m never puts the prefix in PKGNAME.
  devel/apel           tests EMACS_TYPE to choose its packing list,
                       and EMACS_TYPE is what the user asked for.
                       Today that is always what gets built, so it is
                       right by accident; once a package can fall back
                       it is not: with EMACS_TYPE=emacs20 apel builds
                       for emacs29 and is given the Emacs 19 list.
                       EMACS_VERSION_MAJOR is the version in use and
                       six other packages already test that.  It is
                       the only package of the 87 that tests
                       EMACS_TYPE in a condition.

devel/rsltc has a caveat: it wants devel/gentle, which pkgsrc marks
broken on LP64, and the only reason it reached the packing list here
is a gentle I had built with that line removed.  gentle builds on
amd64 11.0 with K&R-era warnings, turns all eleven of its grammars
into C, and rsltc works against it:

  $ rsltc HELLO.rsl
  Checking HELLO ...
  rsltc completed: 0 error(s) 0 warning(s)
  $ rsltc bad.rsl
  bad.rsl:3:13: syntax error

On i386 -- 9.4 and 11.0, both throwaway VMs -- gentle builds and dies
on the first grammar with a segfault.  So the line has it backwards,
but I am not asking you to change it on three data points and no
backtrace; since i386 is not marked, devel/rsltc is failing there in
any bulk build today, and that is also what keeps it out of the one
on amd64.

Four more CONFLICTS match nothing today and cost nothing, but what
they guard may still be true: devel/pcl-cvs (emacs-21.*),
inputmethod/skk (ddskk-[0-9]*), mail/mew (mew-xemacs-[0-9]*) and
tamago-tsunagi's tamago-anthy-[0-9]* (only in wip).  I checked each
with pkg_admin pmatch rather than by eye -- mail/elm's looked wrong
and is fine, its PKGNAME is elm-2.5.8nb5.  No diff for those, since I
do not know what they were for.

And three I looked at and did not fix, because each is a maintainer's
call rather than a bug with one answer:

  devel/ecb            cannot be built by anything.  With
                       GITHUB_PROJECT= ecb it gets past the fetch and
                       stops at "Symbol's function definition is void:
                       semantic-mode", which ecb-2.50's own Makefile
                       writes into the compile script; with
                       xemacs-nox11-21.5.36 and xemacs-packages-1.19
                       (fboundp 'semantic-mode) is nil bare and after
                       (require 'semantic), and the package accepts
                       xemacs214/215 and nothing else.
                       editors/xemacs-packages already installs ECB,
                       63 files under lisp/ecb.
  misc/elscreen        accepts emacs20 and includes apel's
                       buildlink3.mk at top level; apel takes emacs29
                       and up and names emacs20 in
                       EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE.  The dependency is
                       real (elscreen.el requires alist and static),
                       so there is no version where the two meet.  Its
                       own comment says to try wip/elscreen-current.
  misc/bbdb3           CONFLICTS+= xemacs-packages-[0-9]* stopped a
                       build here: mail/wl-snapshot wants bbdb and
                       pkg_add refused it because xemacs-packages was
                       installed.  bbdb3 accepts no xemacs, so it
                       cannot write under lib/xemacs; I compared the
                       two packing lists, 38 files and 7840, and not
                       one name is shared.  Is that line left from
                       when bbdb3 accepted xemacs, or is there a
                       collision I am not seeing?

[fixes diffs]


3. Adaptations -- right today, wrong once the prefix lands
==========================================================

Six diffs, and all of one kind: something rests on ${PKGBASE}, and
the prefix moves PKGBASE.  Six variables under mk/ default to it --
PKGWILDCARD and PKG_SYSCONFVAR in bsd.pkg.mk, FILEBASE in
pkgformat/pkg/package.mk, GITHUB_PROJECT, GITLAB_PROJECT and
CODEBERG_PROJECT in mk/fetch.

  devel/zig-mode
  devel/lua-mode
  misc/emacs-neotree
  textproc/markdown-mode
                       fetch from GitHub without GITHUB_PROJECT, so
                       the fetch asks for
                       github.com/ziglang/emacs30-zig-mode and 404s,
                       and WRKSRC points where the archive does not
                       unpack.  Ten of the 87 fetch from GitHub; these
                       four are the ones that lack the line.  None
                       uses gitlab or codeberg.
  devel/doxymacs       under emacs20 or emacs21 asks for w3-[0-9]*,
                       and www/w3 becomes emacs20-w3.  Only under
                       emacs20, which is why a default sweep goes
                       straight past it.
  mail/mew             passes ${EMACS_ETCPREFIX} to its build and
                       writes share/mew in its PLIST, so the etc move
                       splits the two.  math/ess spells the variable
                       on both sides and needs nothing.

There is a second, larger class I have not touched: 24 of the 87 do
not put EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX in PKGNAME at all -- some set PKGNAME in
options.mk, some not at all -- and gain nothing from the change until
they do.  I took one, misc/color-theme, added the line and built it,
and it broke twice over: MASTER_SITES is .../releases/${PKGBASE}/ and
404s, and the install writes ELISPDIR=${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE}
while the PLIST says color-theme.  Three of the 24 spell ${PKGBASE}
themselves (color-theme, mailutils, emacs-w3m) and are the ones to
look at first; for the other 21 it may well be the one line, but I
would rather not say so until they have been through a build.

[adaptations diffs]


4. How far it got, and what is not measured
============================================

The sweep: of the 57 packages that read modules.mk and do not want
bazel, TeX Live, rust or a gcc bootstrap, on a tree with none of the
fixes applied and the framework dropped in, 36 build and 21 fail --
13 because of the change, 2 behind one of those 13 (semi and
emacs-w3m, waiting on flim), 6 for reasons of their own (three have
lost their distfile: cobol-mode 402, flyspell and ispell-emacs 404;
mu, vm-in-my-copy, and wl-snapshot are above).  Every package named
in 2 and 3 was then built with its diff, and the 13 build.  Two do
not fail and are green in the wrong place -- cqual and tamago-tsunagi,
above -- which only the packing list of the built package shows.

Where: NetBSD 11.0/amd64 for all of it.  apel, flim, zig-mode,
dictem, gnuserv, emacs-w3m, twittering-mode, emacs-dict-client, cqual
and tamago-tsunagi also on i386 11.0 in a throwaway VM, with the same
names and the same directories.  Nothing on another platform.

The info move is the newest part and the least measured.  Built with
it and landing in share/emacs/30.2/info: [INFO-LIST].  Not built:
[INFO-NOT-BUILT], and those 30 are all the packages that install
info, so the rest is by reading.  I would take that part as the one
to look hardest at.

Two things I have not done and would want a word on first: the
site-start.el name, and the 93 shared files between the Emacsen.  And
the four dead CONFLICTS and the bbdb3 line are questions rather than
diffs.

Showta
